---
.title = "AST",
.description = "The flat, data-oriented AST Yuku's parser produces, with its comments and tokens.",
.author = "Arshad Yaseen",
.date = @date("2026-06-23T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---
The parser produces a flat, data-oriented tree. There are no boxed structs and no pointers to chase, every read is a tagged-union switch and a slice index, and `tree.deinit()` frees the whole tree at once.

In JavaScript, [`yuku-parser`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-parser) returns the same tree as an ESTree / TypeScript-ESTree AST, and [`yuku-ast`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-ast) walks, builds, and checks it.

## [Memory layout]($section.id('memory-layout'))

Three arrays in one arena hold the whole tree.

```
Tree
 nodes    every node, its payload and span stored as separate columns
 extras   child lists, as runs of node indices
 strings  text, sliced from the source or interned
```

Four small types carry every reference inside it.

| Type         | Is                                                                           |
| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `NodeIndex`  | A node's position in `nodes`, `.null` for an absent child                    |
| `IndexRange` | A child list, a start and length in `extras`                                 |
| `String`     | Text, a zero-copy slice of the source or an entry in the pool                |
| `Span`       | A source range in bytes                                                      |

## [Reading the tree]($section.id('reading-the-tree'))

`tree.root` is the `program` node, and four calls read everything below it.

```zig
tree.data(idx)    // a node's payload, a tagged union
tree.span(idx)    // its source range
tree.extra(range) // a list of child nodes
tree.string(text) // the bytes of a string
```

A payload is a `switch` away. Tags are snake_case, and [ast.zig](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/yuku/blob/main/src/parser/ast.zig) defines every one with its fields.

```zig
switch (tree.data(idx)) {
    .variable_declaration => |decl| {
        for (tree.extra(decl.declarators)) |declarator| {
            _ = declarator;
        }
    },
    .identifier_reference => |id| {
        std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{tree.string(id.name)});
    },
    else => {},
}
```

Predicates classify a payload in one call: `isExpression`, `isStatement`, `isDeclaration`, `isLiteral`, `isPattern`, `isCallable`, `isIteration`, and `isTypeContext`. To walk the whole tree, use the [traverser](/parser/traverse/).

## [Comments]($section.id('comments'))

The `comments` option collects every comment into `tree.comments`, attaches each to its node, or both. An attached comment records whether it sits `before`, `after`, or `inside` its node, so comments move with their nodes through transforms and [codegen](/parser/codegen/) prints them in place.

```zig
for (tree.commentsOf(idx)) |c| {
    std.debug.print("{t} {s}\n", .{ c.position, tree.string(c.value) });
}
```

## [Tokens]($section.id('tokens'))

`.tokens = true` keeps every token in `tree.tokens`, as the parser resolved it.

```zig
for (tree.tokens) |token| {
    std.debug.print("{t} {s}\n", .{ token.tag, token.text(tree.source) });
}
```

A tag answers what a tool needs, such as `isKeyword()`, `isReserved()`, and `precedence()`, and a token knows whether a line break precedes it, which is what ASI reads. Every node other than `program`, `template_element`, and `jsx_empty_expression` starts and ends on a token boundary, so its tokens are a contiguous run.
